※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4532

※ A variable at the top level lasts as long as the program does, so what it
※ names has to as well.  That is what `static` in the type promises and what
※ nothing else does: a reference borrowed from a parameter lives only as long
※ as the call that made it, and one stored here by that call would outlive what
※ it pointed at.  It is the same question a function answering with a reference
※ is asked, at the other place a value can escape to.
※
※ Written `&mut static u8` this would be allowed, and what is refused is the
※ type as it stands, which promises nothing.

let kept: &mut u8 = ⊥

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6
